That has been my experience as well.  If nothing else, roughly every
other nymph drops a potion of object detection, so by the time you
clean out the mines and get anywhere near the castle or start thinking
about the quest you've got at least two dozen, and there's absolutely
no way you need anywhere near that many of that particular potion.
You will of course want to turn *most* of those into holy water,
because it's useful far more often, but if you use your holy water at
least a little bit judiciously (e.g., take the trouble to fetch your
scrolls of identify from your stash and bless the entire stack at
once, rather than blessing the three you happen to have in your bag of
holding at the moment you decide you need one), you should be able to
spare a small handful of potions for unholy water by the early middle
game.

This is assuming you obtain *some* kind of container relatively early
-- not a bag of holding, necessarily, but something you can use to
carry things in so you don't lose them all the very first time some
random dwarf finds a wand of destroy potions.  If the RNG refuses you
any container at all, you're going to be playing a rather harder game
-- not just because of potions but also scrolls and books and
whatnot.  I suppose conduct players might find it an interesting
change of pace.  "You never applied a container."  Yay.


